 education†learning.parliament.uk】\nL132:   * 【51†Teacher resources and lesson plans†learning.parliament.uk】\nL133:   * 【52†CPD Training†learning.parliament.uk】\nL134:   * 【53†Outreach†learning.parliament.uk】\nL135:   * 【54†Visit†learning.parliament.uk】\nL136: ### Supporting teachers\nL137: \nL138: 【54†Inspire your students Book a school visit, classroom workshop or teacher-training session†learning.parliament.uk】 【55†Free teacher training resources Access videos, worksheets, lesson plans and games.†learning.parliament.uk】\nL139: \nL140: ### Keep up to date\nL141: \nL142:   * 【56†Sign up to our education newsletter†parliament.us16.list-manage.com】\nL143: \nL144: ## Shop\nL145: \nL146: Shop\nL147: \nL148: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL149: \nL150: ### In this section\nL151: \nL152:   * 【3†Shop home†www.shop.parliament.uk】\nL153: \nL154: 【57†UK Parliament】\nL155: # Prorogation\nL156: \nL157: Prorogation (pronounced 'pro-ro-ga-tion') marks the end of a parliamentary session. It is the formal name given to the period between the end of a session of Parliament and the State Opening of Parliament that begins the next session. The parliamentary session may also be prorogued for a short time before Parliament is dissolved, ahead of a general election.\nL158: ## How is prorogation marked?\nL159: \nL160: The King formally prorogues Parliament on the advice of the Privy Council.\nL161: \nL162: 【58†Find out about prorogation in this YouTube explainer video.†www.youtube.com】\nL163: \nL164: Prorogation usually takes the form of an announcement, on behalf of the King, read in the House of Lords. As with the State Opening, it is made to both Houses and the Speaker of the House of Commons and MPs attend the Lords chamber to listen to the speech.\nL165: The same announcement is then read out by the Speaker in the Commons. Following this both the House of Commons and House of Lords are officially prorogued and will not meet again until the State Opening of Parliament.\nL166: ## Prorogation announcement\nL167: \nL168: The prorogation announcement sets out the major bills which have been passed during that session and also describes other measures which have been taken by the government.\nL169: ## What happens to bills still in progress at prorogation?\nL170: \nL171: Prorogation brings to an end nearly all parliamentary business.\nL172: \nL173: However, Public Bills may be carried over from one session to the next, subject to agreement. The first Bill to be treated in this way was the Financial Services and Markets Bill in session 1998-99.\nL174: ## What happens to questions for government departments at prorogation?\nL175: Motions (including early day motions) lapse when the House becomes prorogued, questions which have not been answered fall, nothing more will happen with them. If they have not been answered then they will stay unanswered. No motions or questions can be tabled during a prorogation.\nL176: On the occasions when departments are unable to answer questions substantively before prorogation, ministers provide a standard answer worded as follows: ‘It has not proved possible to respond to the [Right] hon Member in the time available before Prorogation.\nL177: ## Further information\nL178: \nL179:   * 【59†Living Heritage: Prorogation】\nL180:   * 【60†About: State Opening】\nL181: \nL182: \nL183: \nL184: ## See also\nL185: \nL186:   * 【61†Parliamentary sessions and sittings】\nL187:   * 【60†State Opening of Parliament】\nL188:   * 【62†Recall of Parliament】\nL189:   * 【63†Parliamentary occasions】\nL190: ## Related information\nL191: \nL192: What is the difference between a parliament and a session?\nL193: \nL194: A parliament can last a maximum of five years and runs from one general election to the next.\nL195: \nL196: A session usually lasts a year, beginning and ending in the spring. A Parliament is usually made up of several sessions. A session begins with the State Opening and ends with prorogation.\nL197: The State Opening of Parliament marks the formal start of each parliamentary session, with the King's Speech that sets out the government’s agenda for the coming year, outlining proposed policies and legislation.\nL198: \nL199:   * 【60†About Parliament: State Opening of Parliament】\nL200: ## Glossary\nL201: \nL202: An alphabetical list of parliamentary terms with definitions.\nL203: \nL204: 【64†View glossary】\nL205: \nL206: ###### Popular\nL207: \nL208:   * 【65†What's on in Parliament†whatson.parliament.uk】\nL209:   * 【66†Petitions†petition.parliament.uk】\nL210:   * 【28†Careers at Parliament】\nL211:   * 【67†Commons Media Relations】\nL212:   * 【68†Lords Press Office】\nL213:   * 【69†Contact us】\nL214: \nL215: ###### Research\nL216: \nL217:   * 【70†Commons Library†commonslibrary.parliament.uk】\nL218:   * 【71†Lords Library†lordslibrary.parliament.uk】\nL219:   * 【72†POST†post.parliament.uk】\nL220: ###### Search Parliament\nL221: \nL222: [Input]\nL223: \nL224: [Input]\nL225: \nL226: ###### Watch Parliament Live\nL227: \nL228: 【73†Parliament Live TV†parliamentlive.tv】\nL229: \nL230: ###### Follow Parliament\nL231: \nL232: ###### Legal\nL233: \nL234:   * 【74†Accessibility statement】\nL235:   * 【31†Modern slavery statement】\nL236:   * 【75†About this site】\nL237:   * 【76†Privacy notice】\nL238:   * 【77†Cookie policy】\nL239:   * 【2†Cookie settings】\nL240: \nL241: © UK Parliament 2026\n"
